WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.648
A heap allocation creates storage for an
object or sequence of objects with a

2
00:00:04.728 --> 00:00:05.506
defined size.

3
00:00:05.586 --> 00:00:10.188
The allocation function returns a pointer
value that can designate the beginning of

4
00:00:10.268 --> 00:00:11.046
that storage.

5
00:00:11.126 --> 00:00:14.367
That pointer is useful, but it is not the
whole safety story.

6
00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:18.271
A pointer value by itself does not prove
ownership, lifetime, bounds,

7
00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:20.296
initialization state, or safe use.

8
00:00:20.376 --> 00:00:23.811
It does not automatically carry the number
of bytes allocated,

9
00:00:23.891 --> 00:00:25.965
the number of elements that are valid,

10
00:00:26.045 --> 00:00:28.962
or the component responsible for freeing
the object.

11
00:00:29.042 --> 00:00:31.376
Those facts must be tracked by the
program.

12
00:00:31.456 --> 00:00:34.178
A heap object remains valid only for its
lifetime.

13
00:00:34.258 --> 00:00:38.795
Its lifetime begins after successful
allocation and appropriate construction or

14
00:00:38.875 --> 00:00:40.950
initialization for the intended use.

15
00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:43.493
Its lifetime ends when the owner releases
it.

16
00:00:43.573 --> 00:00:46.554
Pointer values can continue to exist after
that point,

17
00:00:46.634 --> 00:00:48.968
but they no longer designate a live
object.

18
00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:51.446
The allocator has a narrow responsibility:

19
00:00:51.526 --> 00:00:55.026
provide and reclaim dynamic storage
according to its contract.

20
00:00:55.106 --> 00:00:57.699
The application owns the higher-level
policy.

21
00:00:57.779 --> 00:01:01.020
It must decide who can read or write, how
capacity is stored,

22
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.248
whether aliases are allowed, how resizing
works, and when cleanup happens.

23
00:01:05.328 --> 00:01:08.440
A useful mental model separates address
from authority.

24
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.307
Having a pointer value means code can name
storage;

25
00:01:11.387 --> 00:01:13.785
it does not mean code is allowed to access
it.

26
00:01:13.865 --> 00:01:17.430
Safe access depends on context, bounds,
object state, lifetime,

27
00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:21.140
and the ownership contract that the
surrounding API establishes.
